      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "PURPOSE",
          "content": "To compare the efficacy of levosimendan with nitroglycerin in patients with isolated diastolic dysfunction undergoing coronary artery bypass grafting."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PROCEDURE",
          "content": "Thirty patients with isolated diastolic dysfunction undergoing on-pump coronary artery bypass grafting were randomized into 2 groups receiving levosimendan or nitroglycerin infusion. The infusion was started before sternotomy and continued in the postoperative period. Perioperatively, diastolic function was serially evaluated at 3 different time points using echocardiography. N-terminal fragment of pro-B-natriuretic peptide (NT-proBNP) levels were measured in both the groups."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "There was a significant improvement in diastolic function as measured by isovolumic relaxation time (P = 0.0001, P = 0.001) and deceleration time (P = 0.0001, P = 0.0001) in the levosimendan group from the baseline in patients with impaired relaxation. Similarly, tissue Doppler imaging also revealed an improvement from the baseline in patients with a pseudonormal pattern (P = 0.018, P = 0.001). Furthermore, there was a significant improvement in the above parameters when compared with the nitroglycerin group. The NT-proBNP levels also demonstrated a similar pattern between the 2 groups (P = 0.03, P = 0.02) when levosimendan was compared with nitroglycerin in patients with a pseudonormal pattern on echocardiography."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Levosimendan is superior to nitroglycerin in improving diastolic function irrespective of coronary revascularization."
        }
      ]},
